# =====================================================================
# COMP 3200 -- Deep Learning -- team repository ignore rules
# =====================================================================
#
# This file tells Git what NOT to track. It ships complete, and it is
# worth reading once rather than skipping, because two of the rules
# below prevent a mess that does not show up until Week 11 -- by which
# point your repo has a hundred megabytes of downloaded junk in its
# history and no easy way to get it out.
#
# The short version:
#   - Junk that a machine regenerates  -> ignored (this file)
#   - Data you downloaded              -> ignored (re-downloadable)
#   - Anything you are graded on       -> COMMITTED (see the block at
#                                         the bottom before you add a
#                                         new line here)
#
# Adding a rule is easy and hard to notice. If you add one, add a
# comment saying why, and check it against "Do not add these" below.
# =====================================================================


# --- Python ----------------------------------------------------------
# Byte-compiled files. Python rewrites these from your .py source on
# every run, so committing them only creates merge conflicts.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# same as *.pyc, plus the .pyo / .pyd variants
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Virtual environments. If you make one, it is yours and local --
# hundreds of megabytes of installed packages that your teammate's
# machine cannot use anyway. (The course installs with a plain
# `pip install numpy matplotlib`; no venv is required.)
.venv/
venv/
env/


# --- Jupyter ---------------------------------------------------------
# Autosave snapshots that Jupyter writes beside your notebook.
# NOTE: this ignores the *checkpoints folder*, not your notebooks.
# The assembled weekN notebook (forge.ipynb, forms.ipynb, ...) is a
# graded deliverable and must be committed.
.ipynb_checkpoints/


# --- Datasets --------------------------------------------------------
# Every corpus this course uses is downloaded by you, from a public
# source, and is large. It does not belong in Git history: your
# teammates can fetch it in thirty seconds, and once a 30 MB text file
# is committed it stays in the history forever even after you delete
# it.
#
# A bare `data/` with no leading slash matches a folder named `data`
# ANYWHERE in the repo -- so this one line covers week11/data/,
# week12/data/, week14/data/, week15/data/ and final_project/data/.
#
# Where to get each file: see DATASETS.md in the repo root.
data/


# --- Cached arrays and downloaded model data -------------------------
# INFERENCE -- this rule is not stated in any assignment. It is here
# because of how the MNIST loader works, and it is the single cheapest
# line in this file:
#
#   Weeks 8, 9 and 10 each ask you to put a `data.py` loader inside the
#   week folder. It downloads mnist.npz (~11 MB) and caches it *next
#   to your code* -- week8/mnist.npz, week9/mnist.npz, week10/mnist.npz
#   -- not in a data/ folder. Without this line, that is three 11 MB
#   binaries committed by accident, by whichever teammate ran the code
#   first.
#
#   Week 12 adds a fourth: after training you save the four weight
#   tensors with np.savez, which writes a .npz too.
#
# Narrowed to the cache filename on purpose. A blanket *.npz would also
# swallow the weight tensors Week 12 has you save with np.savez, which you
# may well want to keep.
#
# If you ever have an .npz you genuinely want in the repo, commit it on
# purpose with: git add -f path/to/that.npz
mnist.npz


# --- OS junk ---------------------------------------------------------
# Metadata files your operating system scatters into folders. Nobody
# has ever wanted these in a diff.
# macOS
.DS_Store
# Windows
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini


# --- Editor / IDE ----------------------------------------------------
# Per-developer settings. Your teammate's editor config is not your
# business, and vice versa.
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/


# =====================================================================
# DO NOT ADD THESE. Read this before "tidying up" the file.
# =====================================================================
#
# *.png  -- NEVER. Plots are graded deliverables in this course and
#           must be committed. Ignoring images would silently zero
#           several rubric lines:
#             week4/fig1_weights.png, week4/fig2_frozen.png
#             week8/curves_part1.png, week8/curves_part3.png
#             week9/forms_plot.png,   week9/compare_plot.png
#             week10/kernels.png
#           Editors and generic Python .gitignore templates love to
#           suggest this one. Say no.
#
# *.csv  -- NEVER. Week 12 grades "perplexity log saved"; that log is
#           a CSV you commit.
#
# *.ipynb -- NEVER. The assembled notebook is the headline deliverable
#           from Week 8 onward.
#
# *.md / README.md -- NEVER. Documentation is graded every week.
#
# If you are about to add a pattern and you are not certain, run
# `git status` first and see what it would hide. If it hides something
# a rubric names, it is the wrong pattern.
# =====================================================================
